Z||000013||rogergarrison||06-02-2001||08:14 PM||||Guess I just learned something new. Did you form the parts from plexiglas? Ive always been under the assumption that once a plexiglas piece was formed at manufacture, it couldnt be remolded. Only curved with heat. Every piece ive ever tried to alter with some heat just ended up breaking.<P>[QUOTE]Originally posted by Rob Durham:<BR>[B]You can vaccu-form just about anything, 35% of the parts on my hovercraft, I vaccu-formed<BR>  <P><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 06-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||ray b||06-02-2001||09:50 PM||||just heat it and wait till it begins to sag<BR>then vacume it in to a mold, multi part molds for complex shapes add to cost but scoops are eazy use original and just copy it<BR>simple box and shop vac may work to form it<BR>eltic heat 400? deg. or about no flames plexi burns.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000015||Nashco||06-02-2001||10:10 PM||||I know there's enough airflow for the engine compartment to be cool during driving, but wouldn't the plexi start to sag after sitting in 90 degree temps, traffic jam, and high heat under the hood?  We're talking air temps of probably about 250 degrees aren't we?  I'm not saying I know what the figure is, this is a guess.<P>I was hoping you were thinking of making some GT quarter windows.  You have seen the turbo style quarter windows, right?  Well, I would love to have those if you could see through them!<P>Who has made plexi molds?  I'm sure I could do it if I knew how, but I'd imagine it takes a lot of experience to figure out where to put the vacuum port locations to avoid deforming the plexi.  Is it feasible to make plexi in such a compound shape as the turbo GT windows?  I'd be very interested in something like that.  I'd imagine that they would scratch up easy, but once you make a mold, the plexi is cheap.  After a year or two you could just make a couple on the weekend (or order one from somebody for fairly cheap).  Since they're clear, you paint them on the backside with the factory style paint scheme, around the edges, and still use the 3M double sided tape.<P>Who's game?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000016||TRiAD||06-03-2001||10:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B>I know there's enough airflow for the engine compartment to be cool during driving, but wouldn't the plexi start to sag after sitting in 90 degree temps, traffic jam, and high heat under the hood?  We're talking air temps of probably about 250 degrees aren't we?  I'm not saying I know what the figure is, this is a guess.<P>I was hoping you were thinking of making some GT quarter windows.  You have seen the turbo style quarter windows, right?  Well, I would love to have those if you could see through them!<P>Who has made plexi molds?  I'm sure I could do it if I knew how, but I'd imagine it takes a lot of experience to figure out where to put the vacuum port locations to avoid deforming the plexi.  Is it feasible to make plexi in such a compound shape as the turbo GT windows?  I'd be very interested in something like that.  I'd imagine that they would scratch up easy, but once you make a mold, the plexi is cheap.  After a year or two you could just make a couple on the weekend (or order one from somebody for fairly cheap).  Since they're clear, you paint them on the backside with the factory style paint scheme, around the edges, and still use the 3M double sided tape.<P>Who's game?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's very possible. I'll look into it. <P><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com<BR>-----------------<BR>Looking for a white '88 GT 5 sp...<BR>...Could it be...Found?<BR>Stay tuned!</I></B>||||reg||1
Z||000017||FieroFanaticus2||06-03-2001||02:02 PM||||We vaccuum form plexiglass all the time where I work.  I've done it thousands of times for hundreds of different products that we sell.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Nashco||06-03-2001||02:47 PM||||Excellent FieroFanaticus2, so what're you willing to help out with?  Do you know what the turbo windows look like; would it be possible to form them?<P>What kind of temps can plexi take before warping or yellowing?<P>Do you know of any good books or websites that explain the science of plexi vacuuming?<P>What kind of heating equipment would be necessary to make what we're talking about?  I was thinking you could build a large rack, basically a double wide/deep oven.  Just get two old ovens, use both the upper and lower heating coils and all the controls (rigged to be able to use either broil or both broil and cook).  Put all four coils on top, have large sliders on each side, and similar look to a pizza oven.  You would have control for each coil, so you could do small localized heat or the whole area.<P>Would that work, or would a portable unit be better, and put it on above the mold, rather than the mold in a stove?  Or, is this totally retarded?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000019||cadaver||06-03-2001||03:00 PM||||First there is better stuff to try to vacuform than "plexi". There are plasctic that are designed to be thermo formed. And yes you can get very complex shapes. If you try to do a scoop be sure to pack the bottom with something if you vacuum has any kind of juice it will crush them.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Rob Durham||06-03-2001||03:36 PM||||You have to heat the plexi or acrylic evenly in an oven @375 deg. untill it sags about 2" in the center, then you put it on the mold and suck it down (simple terms).<P>The majority of molds for vaccu-forming are not like fiberglass molds where you lay up glass in the mold you actually pull the plexi down over the mold and you remove the part within miniutes of forming it because once the plexi cools it will lock itself on to the mold.<P>I have done center consols and dash pannels for boats, paddel boats, hovercraft parts, bumpers for semi's, holsters for guns among other items.<P>"What kind of heating equipment would be necessary to make what we're talking about? I was thinking you could build a large rack, basically a double wide/deep oven. Just get two old ovens, use both the upper and lower heating coils and all the controls (rigged to be able to use either broil or both broil and cook). Put all four coils on top, have large sliders on each side, and similar look to a pizza oven. You would have control for each coil, so you could do small localized heat or the whole area."<P>If your making small parts you can use your house hold oven and an inexpensive vaccu-table. Check out this site <A HREF="http://www.warmplastic.com/pri.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.warmplastic.com/pri.html</A> <P>Rob<p>[This message has been edited by Rob Durham (edited 06-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||Pontiaddict||06-03-2001||08:13 PM||||How about making an Indy style scoop out of plexi? That way, you could see through it and therefore see behind you somewhat. Yes, it would be complicated, and then even though they were clear, you couldn't see through them, but it would still be cool. ||||reg||1
Z||000022||87GTZ34||06-03-2001||09:54 PM||||BTW - Most electrical (back lit) signs that you see all over the place are vacuum formed plexi and then painted from the back.  The molds that are used are typically made from wood as they are not precision tolerance items.  As Rob mentioned, the possiblities are endless in custom car work.||||reg||1
Z||000023||fyerstarter||06-03-2001||10:08 PM||||Triad, <P>I don't understand you. First you post asking if there is anyone who can mold plexiglass for you and then in the same post you advertise your intent to sell these scoops. <P>So then do you expect it will work something like this.<P>Whichever forum member you find to do this makes the scoops and then sells them to you.<P>Then you jack up the price and sell them to us?<P>Why would we not just go direct to the source and skip the middleman(you)?<P><BR>No offense but just sounds to me like your trying to put some extra cash in your pocket at other peoples expense. You neither designed this scoop nor will you be manufacturing it. ||||reg||1
